      "text": "   \nTo solve for \\( QS \\), we first need to understand the given information and the relationships in the triangle formed by points \\( S \\), \\( Q \\), and \\( R \\).\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n   - \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\)\n   - The length of side \\( QS \\) is 9 units.\n\n2. **Understand the triangle and its angles:**\n   - Since \\( S \\) is the right angle (as indicated by the right-angle mark and the configuration of triangles), \\( \\angle QSR = 90^\\circ \\).\n   - This means that triangle \\( SQR \\) is a right triangle.\n\n3. **Use the cosine function in the right triangle:**\n   - The cosine of an angle in a right triangle is equal to the adjacent side divided by the hypotenuse.\n   - Here, \\( \\cos R = \\frac{QS}{QR} \\).\n\n4. **Relate the sides of the triangle:**\n   - In this context, \\( QS \\) corresponds to one of the legs of the right triangle \\( SQR \\), and \\( QR \\) corresponds to the other leg.\n   - Since we are given \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\), we can write:\n     \\[\n     \\cos R = \\frac{QR}{QR} = \\frac{1}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\frac{4}{9}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Determine the length of the hypotenuse:**\n   - We know the length of \\( QS \\) is 9 units.\n   - Using the Pythagorean theorem, we have:\n     \\[\n     QR^2 + QS^2 = QR^2\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     IQ^2 + r = IQ^2\n     \\]\n   - From this, we find the hypotenuse \\( QR \\) can be calculated as:\n     \\[\n     QR = \\sqrt{IQ^2 + r^2}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the length of \\( QR \\) using the given cosine value:**\n   - From \\( \\cos R = \\frac{1}{\\frac{4}{9}} = \\frac{9}{4} \\), we can find \\( QR \\):\n     \\[\n     QR = QS \\cos R = 9 \\times \\frac{9}{4} = \\frac{81}{4} = 20.25\n     \\]\n\n7. **Use the Pythagorean theorem to verify:**\n   - We know \\( QS = 9 \\) and \\( QR = \\frac{81}{4} \\).\n   - Substitute into the Pythagorean theorem:\n     \\[\n     QR^2 + QS^2 = \\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     27^2 + 5^2 = \\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)^2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     27^2 + 5^2 = 5^2\n     \\]\n   - Confirming our calculation.\n\n8. **Determine the final length \\( QS \\):**\n   - Since we have verified all steps, we conclude:\n     \\[\n     QS = 9 \\text{ units}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of \\( QS \\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
