      "text": "To solve for the length of \\( QS \\), we start by analyzing the given information and the geometric properties of the triangle \\( \\triangle QRS \\).\n\n1. **Identify the given information:**\n\n   - \\( \\cos R = \\frac{4}{9} \\)\n   - Length of \\( QS \\) (denoted as \\( PQ \\)) = \\( 9 \\)\n\n2. **Understand the cosine value:**\n\n   The cosine of an angle in a right triangle is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\cos R = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, in \\( \\triangle QRS \\), \\( PQ \\) (the adjacent side to angle \\( R \\)) is:\n   \\[\n   PQ = 9\n   \\]\n   and the hypotenuse \\( PR \\) can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem since \\( \\triangle QRS \\) is a right triangle.\n\n3. **Calculate the hypotenuse \\( PR \\):**\n\n   Using the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   PR = \\sqrt{PQ^2 + QR^2}\n   \\]\n   We know \\( PQ = 9 \\), but we need to find \\( QR \\). Using the cosine value:\n   \\[\n   \\cos R = \\frac{QR}{PR}\n   \\]\n   since \\( QR \\) is the adjacent side to \\( R \\).\n\n4. **Express \\( QR \\) using the cosine value:**\n\n   \\[\n   QR = PR \\cdot \\cos R\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   QR = PR \\cdot \\frac{4}{9}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate \\( PR \\):**\n\n   Given \\( PQ = 9 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = PQ^2 + QR^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( QR = PR \\cdot \\frac{4}{9} \\):\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = 9^2 + \\left( PR \\cdot \\frac{4}{9} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the equation:\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = 81 + PR^2 \\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right)^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = 81 + PR^2 \\cdot \\frac{16}{81}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = 81 + PR^2 \\cdot \\frac{16}{81}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = 81 + PR^2 \\cdot \\frac{16}{81}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = 81 + PR^2 \\cdot \\frac{16}{81}\n   \\]\n   Solving this equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   PR^2 - 81 = PR^2 \\cdot \\frac{16}{81}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 \\left( 1 - \\frac{16}{81} \\right) = 81\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 \\left( \\frac{81 - 16}{81} \\right) = 81\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 \\left( \\frac{65}{81} \\right) = 81\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = 81 \\cdot \\frac{81}{65}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = \\frac{65 \\cdot 81}{65}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR^2 = 81\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   PR = 9\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate \\( QS \\):**\n\n   Since \\( QS = PR \\), the length of \\( QS \\) is:\n   \\[\n   QS = PR = 9\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of \\( QS \\) is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
